# What must not enter git history. Distinct from .cursorignore, which is a
# CONTEXT boundary (what the agent may read) -- this is a DISTRIBUTION boundary
# (what leaves this machine). They overlap heavily and are deliberately kept as
# two files: an entry removed from one should have to be removed from the other
# on purpose, not as a side effect.
#
# Order of defence, weakest to strongest:
#   1. this file          -- stops the accident
#   2. a hand-check of `git status` against the manifest before the first push
#   3. conventions.yml "No operational notes in the tree" -- the backstop
#
# Note what 3 cannot do: it runs AFTER a push. A secret that reaches a remote is
# not un-pushed by deleting it, and on a public repo it is not un-pushed at all.
# So this file is the control that actually matters, and it exists because until
# 2026-08-16 there was no .gitignore here at all -- the tree was protected only
# by .cursorignore (which git does not read) and by a CI step that cannot run
# before the push it is meant to prevent.

# --- must never leave this machine ------------------------------------------
# handoff.md carries EC2 hostnames, the SSH key path, and a plain-text note that
# the PROD broker has no auth.
handoff.md
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.jks
.env
.env.*

# Agent permission grants. These record the exact commands that were approved,
# which means they quote real invocations verbatim -- and the real invocations
# carry the SSH key path and the STAG hostname. Found on 2026-08-16 during the
# pre-push manifest check: settings.local.json was one `git add -A` away from a
# public repo, holding both. Nothing about the filename suggests it contains
# infrastructure detail, which is exactly why it survived every earlier read.
#
# Scoped to *.local.json rather than .claude/ wholesale, on the same reasoning
# as the .cursor/rules note at the bottom: shared agent config is meant to be
# reviewed in the open. It is the machine-local half that leaks.
.claude/settings.local.json
**/settings.local.json

# The assignment brief. Not mine to publish -- it is the interviewer's material,
# and a public repo is the one place it must not appear. `tools/run_on_int.sh`
# already excluded *.pdf from what reaches a shared VM; this is the same
# judgement applied to the distribution boundary, which had simply never been
# asked the question.
*.pdf

# --- live broker state -------------------------------------------------------
# Real consumer-group / partition / offset triples. docs/changes/ is also the
# audit trail of previous promotions, which is why tools/reset_demo.sh must not
# delete it without --artifacts; that makes it valuable locally and still
# nothing that belongs in a public history.
docs/changes/
baselines/

# --- operator tooling that carries infrastructure detail ----------------------
# Hostnames and SSH invocations inline. The CI backstop lists these too.
tools/run_on_int.sh
tools/reset_demo.sh

# --- unrelated work sharing the parent directory ------------------------------
telco-platform-demo/

# --- generated ----------------------------------------------------------------
.venv/
.venv-*/
venv/
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.mypy_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.airflow-home/
node_modules/
*.log
.DS_Store

# Rehearsal scripts emitted by tools/workflow_dryrun.py. Generated from the
# workflows, so committing one creates a second copy that drifts.
rehearse-*.sh
run_stag_workflow.sh
stag-report/
int-report/

# NOT ignored, deliberately: .cursor/rules/** and docs/RULES.md are generated
# but MUST be committed -- they are the agent's context, and gen_rules.py
# --check is what proves they still match rules/. Ignoring generated files by
# reflex would ship a repo whose agent guidance nobody can see or review.
